{%- comment -%}
  CollectionPage structured data for the pages that argue a category rather than
  sell a variant: the waterproof-socks hub, the wudu page and the four use-case
  pages.

  These live at /pages/ rather than /collections/ because the catalogue is a
  single SKU and a Shopify collection of one product would add a URL without
  adding an answer. The schema type still says CollectionPage, because the type
  describes what the page *does* for a reader — sets out a category and points
  at what is in it — not which Shopify object rendered it.

  Every id is built from `shop.url`, so the graph re-points itself the moment a
  custom domain is connected and nothing has to be found and edited by hand.

  `about` and the ItemList both name the product these pages describe, which is
  what ties the set together as one catalogue instead of six loose pages. The
  product comes from a theme setting rather than a handle typed in here.

  BreadcrumbList is not emitted here — the breadcrumb section already emits it
  from the same array it draws, so the trail and its markup cannot disagree.
  FAQPage is not emitted here either; the question section owns that.

  Accepts:
  - defined_term: {Boolean} Also emit a DefinedTerm for the entity this page
    defines. Wudu only, where being the source of the definition is the point.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign schema_product = settings.schema_product
  assign page_url = shop.url | append: page.url
-%}
<script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "CollectionPage",
    "@id": "{{ page_url }}#page",
    "url": "{{ page_url }}",
    "name": {{ page.title | json }},
    {%- if page_description != blank -%}
      "description": {{ page_description | strip_html | json }},
    {%- endif -%}
    "inLanguage": "en-GB",
    "isPartOf": { "@id": "{{ shop.url }}/#website" },
    {%- if schema_product != blank -%}
      "about": { "@id": "{{ shop.url }}{{ schema_product.url }}#product" },
      "mainEntity": {
        "@type": "ItemList",
        "itemListElement": [
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 1,
            "url": "{{ shop.url }}{{ schema_product.url }}"
          }
        ]
      },
    {%- endif -%}
    "publisher": { "@id": "{{ shop.url }}/#organization" }
  }
</script>

{%- comment -%}
  The entity definition. This states what the thing *is*, in the same words the
  visible page uses — which is the whole basis on which it may be published at
  all. It makes a claim about the sock's physical properties and no claim about
  religious validity, permissibility, certification or approval, because schema
  is ingested directly by language models and a claim made here travels further,
  and is harder to retract, than the same claim in body copy.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if defined_term -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "DefinedTerm",
      "@id": "{{ page_url }}#term",
      "name": "Wudhu socks",
      "alternateName": ["Wudu socks", "Ablution socks", "Masah socks"],
      "description": "Waterproof socks built to the physical properties the masah conditions rest on: water does not pass through them, they hold their shape rather than collapsing against the foot, and they stay in place through normal wear.",
      "inDefinedTermSet": "{{ shop.url }}/pages/how-to-make-masah",
      "mainEntityOfPage": { "@id": "{{ page_url }}#page" }
    }
  </script>
{%- endif -%}
